registerClass12ExerciseBanks("eng", {"1":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u01-activities-a",1]],"d":"eng12-u01-ipa-activity-a","s":["Left column: /ɑ:m/ arm; /ɪə/ ear; /aɪ/ eye; /feɪs/ face; /fʊt/ foot; /heə/ hair; /hænd/ hand; /maʊθ/ mouth; /nəʊz/ nose; /'ʃəʊldə/ shoulder.","Right column: kəʊm comb; ʃæm'pu: shampoo; 'neɪlfaɪl nail file; pə'fju:m perfume; 'taʊəl towel; 'ʃeɪvə shaver; səʊp soap; heə'draɪə hair dryer; 'tu:θpeɪst toothpaste; spreɪ spray."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-activities-a",2],["eng12-u01-activities-a",3],["eng12-u01-activities-a",4],["eng12-u01-activities-a",5],["eng12-u01-activities-a",6],["eng12-u01-activities-a",7],["eng12-u01-activities-a",8],["eng12-u01-activities-a",9],["eng12-u01-activities-a",10],["eng12-u01-activities-a",11],["eng12-u01-activities-a",12],["eng12-u01-activities-a",13],["eng12-u01-activities-a",14],["eng12-u01-activities-b",0],["eng12-u01-activities-b",1],["eng12-u01-activities-b",2],["eng12-u01-activities-b",3],["eng12-u01-activities-b",4],["eng12-u01-activities-b",5],["eng12-u01-activities-b",6],["eng12-u01-activities-b",7],["eng12-u01-activities-b",8]],"n":"In items 6 and 8 the verb in brackets is cut off in the scan; save→spend and hit→missed fit the sentences and the word box.","s":["1. laughed (opposite of cry)","2. emptied (fill)","3. forget (remember)","4. succeeded (fail)","5. leave (arrive)","6. spend (save)","7. fallen (rise)","8. missed (hit)","9. failed (pass)","10. lose (win)","11. admitted (deny)","12. rejected (accept)","13. received (send)","14. lend (borrow)","15. taught (learn)","16. banned (allow)","17. loosened (tighten)","18. defend (attack)","19. destroyed (create)","20. punished (reward)"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",2]],"s":["At Uhud the Holy Prophet (ﷺ) consulted the young Companions as well as the elders, and he accepted the opinion of the young men who wanted to go out of Madinah and meet the enemy in the open. He also gave young Companions important positions in the battle, which shows the trust and respect he gave to the youth."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",3]],"s":["The Holy Prophet (ﷺ) said that the example of himself and the prophets before him is like a man who built a beautiful house but left the place of one brick in a corner empty. People went round it, admired it and said, \"If only this brick were put in its place!\" He said: \"I am that brick, and I am the last of the prophets\" (Bukhari, Muslim). The hadith shows the finality of prophethood."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",4]],"s":["He spent his youth in honesty, purity and hard work. He grazed sheep, worked as a trader with Hazrat Khadijah's (رضی اللہ عنہا) goods, and was so truthful and trustworthy that people called him al-Sadiq and al-Amin. He kept away from the idol worship, drinking and games of the age of ignorance and helped the poor and the weak."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",5]],"s":["Many young people today are influenced by materialism, the media and fashion; they waste time, neglect prayer and respect for elders, and some fall into drugs, dishonesty and immodesty. In the light of the Prophet's (ﷺ) teachings youth should be truthful, modest, hard-working and kind, use their energy for learning and serving people, and make his Seerah their model."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",6]],"s":["In his youth he joined Hilf al-Fudul, a pact of the young men of Makkah to help the oppressed and protect travellers and the weak. He helped in the rebuilding of the Ka'bah and settled the dispute over placing the Black Stone peacefully, and he took part in trade and in helping relatives, orphans and the needy."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",7]],"s":["He said that modesty (haya) is a part of faith and that it brings nothing but good. For young people modesty protects their character: it keeps them away from indecency, controls their eyes, tongue and dress, and earns them respect in society and the pleasure of Allah."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",8]],"s":["He lived a very simple life: he slept on a mat of palm leaves that left marks on his body, mended his own clothes and shoes, helped in household work, and many days no fire was lit in his house for cooking — the family lived on dates and water. (Any one such instance from the text is a correct answer.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-comprehension",9]],"s":["He wanted to see youth who are strong in faith, truthful, modest and hard-working; who worship Allah from a young age (one of the seven under Allah's shade is a young man who grew up in His worship); who serve their parents and society, seek knowledge and are ready to sacrifice for Islam and the nation."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u01-writing-c",7]],"s":["Sample outline — The Love of the Holy Prophet (ﷺ) for the Youth of the Ummah: I. Introduction — youth as the strength of a nation; the Prophet's (ﷺ) special care for young people. II. Trust and responsibility — young Companions given leadership (Usamah bin Zaid commanding an army, Mus'ab bin Umair as teacher in Madinah, Ali (رضی اللہ عنہ) on the night of Hijrah). III. Consultation and respect — accepting the opinion of the youth at Uhud. IV. Training and guidance — teaching manners to the young (\"say Bismillah, eat with your right hand\"), the hadith of seven under Allah's shade. V. Lessons for today — modesty, truthfulness, learning and service. VI. Conclusion."]},{"q":[["eng12-u01-writing-c",9]],"s":["Possible group findings — weaknesses: misuse of mobile phones and social media, wasting time, neglect of prayer and studies, disrespect to elders, impatience, imitation of foreign culture, drug addiction. Remedies: following the Seerah, good company, guidance by parents and teachers, sports and healthy activities, time management, religious and moral education, and positive role models."]}]}},"2":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u02-writing-vocab-a",10],["eng12-u02-writing-vocab-a",11],["eng12-u02-writing-vocab-a",12],["eng12-u02-writing-vocab-a",13],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u02-vocab-b",8]],"n":"Item 4 is cut off in the scan; the remaining words show an explanation clue.","s":["1. vociferous — antonym clue (\"doesn't talk much anymore\")","2. pedagogical — example clue (high schools, kindergartens, colleges)","3. parsimonious — explanation clue (refused to give pennies; it hurt him to part with money)","4. explanation clue (the meaning is explained after the dash/comma)","5. clandestine — antonym clue (\"quite openly\")","6. ecclesiastics — example clue (priests, ministers, pastors)","7. churlish — synonym clue (rude, sullen, ill-mannered)","8. conflagration — explanation clue (every building completely burned)","9. fallacious — synonym clue (misleading, plain wrong)","10. loquacious — antonym clue (\"others hardly talk at all\")","11. somnolent — explanation clue (needs medication to stay awake)","12. celestial — example clue (sun, moon and stars)"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",1]],"s":["The Quaid said the Constituent Assembly had two main functions: first, to frame the future constitution of Pakistan, and second, to work as a full and sovereign legislative body (the Federal Legislature) for the country until the constitution was made."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",2]],"s":["He meant the creation of two independent states, Pakistan and India, in the subcontinent. He called it unprecedented because there was no parallel in history of such a vast change — the creation of a new state by peaceful democratic struggle and the transfer of power over so many millions of people."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",3]],"s":["He called bribery and corruption a poison and one of the biggest curses, which must be put down with an iron hand. He also condemned black-marketing as a great crime, and called nepotism and jobbery an evil inherited from the past that he would not tolerate."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",4]],"s":["He said that many people did not agree with the partition of India, but in his opinion there was no other solution; a united India would never have worked and would have led to disaster. Partition was a final settlement that both sides should honour, and history would prove it right."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",5]],"s":["In the Quaid's view the biggest hindrance was the class and communal division — the division between Hindus and Muslims (and the castes among Hindus); without these divisions India would have won freedom long ago."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",6]],"s":["He assured the minorities of full equality: every citizen, whatever his religion, caste or creed, would have equal rights, privileges and obligations. Minorities would be protected and free to practise their religion; the state would not discriminate against anyone on the basis of faith."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",7]],"s":["He envisioned freedom of religion and worship, equality of citizenship, freedom from fear, corruption and exploitation — \"You are free to go to your temples, mosques or any other places of worship\", with religion being a personal matter of the individual and not the business of the state."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",8]],"s":["He did not mean that people would give up their faith. He meant that in the political sense, as citizens of the state, people would not be treated as Hindus or Muslims; religious identity would remain a personal matter, while all would be equal citizens of Pakistan."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",9]],"s":["His vision was of a democratic, welfare state based on justice, equality and fair play; free from corruption, bribery and nepotism; where all citizens, including minorities, have equal rights and religious freedom; and where the people work with unity, faith and discipline for the progress and prosperity of the nation."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",10]],"s":["His guiding principles were justice and complete impartiality — he said he would be guided by fair play and would not be swayed by any political or party prejudice in his position as President, and he asked for the cooperation of all for this. Significance: these principles are the foundation of good government — they build trust in the state, protect the rights of all citizens, unite the nation and make the rule of law possible."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u02-comprehension",12]],"s":["Debate points — the Pakistan the Quaid wanted: a democratic state where sovereignty belongs to Allah and authority is used by the people's representatives; based on the principles of Islam — justice, equality and brotherhood; a welfare state free from corruption, bribery, black-marketing and nepotism; equal rights and religious freedom for minorities; unity, faith and discipline as national values; economic independence and education. Each group should support its arguments with quotations from the Quaid's speeches (11 August 1947 and others)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u02-writing-vocab-a",7]],"s":["Sample essay — Quaid-e-Azam was a far-sighted political leader. (Hook) Few leaders in history created a nation and a state at the same time. (Thesis) The Quaid's speech of 11 August 1947 shows his far-sightedness. Body: (1) He saw that a united India would not have worked and accepted partition as the only solution. (2) He named the dangers the new state would face — bribery, corruption, black-marketing, nepotism — and asked for them to be crushed with an iron hand. (3) He knew that a stable state needs the trust of all citizens, so he promised equal rights and religious freedom to minorities. (4) He gave the Assembly a double role — making the constitution and governing the country. Conclusion: His vision of a just, democratic and united Pakistan is still the best guide for the nation."]}]}},"3":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",16]],"d":"eng12-u03-ipa","n":"Part A's word list is not legible in this scan; only the instruction is recorded.","s":["Vocabulary A — use a dictionary to give the meaning of each listed word and use it in a sentence of your own (answers will vary)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-b",0],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",1],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",2],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",3],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",4],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",5],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",6],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",7],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",8],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",9],["eng12-u03-exercises-b",10]],"s":["1. have been waiting","2. paying","3. had left","4. visited","5. attacked","6. sought","7. forsook","8. fled","9. left","10. struck"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u03-about-glossary",4]],"s":["The theme is the power of music and of a beautiful moment in nature to touch the human heart and live on in memory. A simple Highland girl's song, though the poet cannot understand its words, fills him with deep feeling and stays with him long after he has heard it."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-about-glossary",5]],"s":["He describes it as a melancholy (sad) strain that overflows the whole valley; sweeter than the song of the nightingale welcoming weary travellers in the Arabian desert, and more thrilling than the cuckoo's song in spring breaking the silence of the far Hebrides islands."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",0]],"s":["In the second stanza the poet is enchanted and thrilled. He feels the song is more welcome than the nightingale's song to tired travellers and more thrilling than the cuckoo's voice in the lonely seas — he is filled with wonder and delight."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",1]],"s":["The first stanza shows a lonely Highland girl in a wide field, cutting and binding the grain by herself and singing a sad song; the poet asks the passer-by to stop or pass gently, and the valley is full of the overflowing sound of her song."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",2]],"s":["Examples: \"single in the field\" / \"Stop here, or gently pass\" (s-sounds); \"sings a melancholy strain\" (s); \"breaking the silence of the seas\" (s); \"cuts and binds the grain\"; \"farthest Hebrides\"."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",3]],"s":["In the third stanza the poet imagines what she may be singing about: perhaps old, unhappy, far-off things and battles long ago, or some ordinary matter of today — some natural sorrow, loss or pain that has been and may be again. We picture a simple girl expressing universal human sorrow."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",4]],"s":["In the last stanza the poet says that whatever the theme was, the girl sang as if her song would never end; he saw her singing over her sickle as she worked, and he listened, motionless and still. As he climbed up the hill, he carried the music in his heart long after it could no longer be heard."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",5]],"s":["The dominant theme is the lasting effect of beauty: an ordinary experience of nature and human feeling — a reaper's song — becomes a permanent joy and memory in the poet's heart; it also shows the universal language of music that touches us even without understanding its words."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",12]],"s":["Sample paragraph: In 'The Solitary Reaper' Wordsworth uses simple, musical language and vivid imagery. The opening imperatives (\"Behold her\", \"Stop here, or gently pass\") draw the reader into the scene. Alliteration (\"single in the field\", \"silence of the seas\") gives the poem a soft, singing quality, while the comparisons with the nightingale and the cuckoo (similes and contrasts) express the sweetness of her song. Rhetorical questions in the third stanza (\"Will no one tell me what she sings?\") show the poet's curiosity, and the regular rhyme and rhythm imitate a song. The final stanza moves from present to past tense, showing how the experience has become a lasting memory."]},{"q":[["eng12-u03-exercises-a",14]],"s":["Debate points — rural life: peace, fresh air, healthy food, simple living, close relationships, natural beauty. Urban life: better education, health care, jobs, transport, technology and entertainment. Each group should give examples and answer the other side's points; the teacher can conclude that both have advantages and that balance is best."]}]}},"4":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u04-exercises-a",6]],"d":"eng12-u04-dict-ipa","s":["person /'pɜ:sn/; once /wʌns/; police /pə'li:s/; lose /lu:z/; position /pə'zɪʃn/; industry /'ɪndəstri/; major /'meɪdʒə/; build /bɪld/; language /'læŋgwɪdʒ/; international /ˌɪntə'næʃnəl/; else /els/; center /'sentə/; enough /ɪ'nʌf/; calm /kɑ:m/; color /'kʌlə/; lure /lʊə/; knife /naɪf/","information /ˌɪnfə'meɪʃn/; question /'kwestʃən/; business /'bɪznəs/; power /'paʊə/; change /tʃeɪndʒ/; move /mu:v/; book /bʊk/; development /dɪ'veləpmənt/; young /jʌŋ/; national /'næʃnəl/; water /'wɔ:tə/; yet /jet/; perhaps /pə'hæps/; until /ən'tɪl/; control /kən'trəʊl/; include /ɪn'klu:d/; believe /bɪ'li:v/"]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-exercises-a",7],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",8],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",9],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",10],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",11],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",12],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",13],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",14],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",15],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",16],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",17],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",18],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",19],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",20],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",21],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",22],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",23],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",24],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",0],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",1],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",2],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",3]],"s":["1. catch (done)","2. save","3. departs","4. filled","5. borrowed","6. forbids","7. denied","8. set","9. public","10. failed","11. punish","12. receive","13. bright","14. defend","15. permanent","16. guilty","17. absent","18. voluntary","19. minor","20. weak"]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-exercises-b",4],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",5],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",6],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",7],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",8],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",9],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",10],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",11],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",12],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",13],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",14],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",15],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",16],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",17],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",18],["eng12-u04-exercises-b",19]],"n":"Clue 11 is printed as \"happening every November\"; novennial actually means happening every ninth year. Clue 4 repeats the idea of clue 3, so either fraternity or union is accepted.","s":["1. urbanite","2. revive","3. fraternity","4. fraternity / union (friendship and brotherhood)","5. decapitate","6. patriotism","7. homonym","8. dentist","9. dual","10. victim","11. novennial","12. popular","13. quadruped","14. union"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",2]],"s":["The writer links the decorated trucks of today with the camel caravans of the past: just as camel drivers once decorated their camels with bells, beads and colourful cloth for long journeys on the trade routes, truck drivers now decorate their trucks, which have taken the place of camels as the carriers of goods across the land."]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",3]],"s":["She is illustrating that truck art has regional styles: trucks from Peshawar, for example, are decorated with wooden carvings and particular motifs, while those from Rawalpindi are known for plastic work and reflective tape — each region has its own distinctive taste."]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",4]],"s":["She shows that truck art is a skilled craft done by specialists — painters, carpenters, metal-workers and calligraphers — each working on a different part of the truck, so decorating a truck is a team effort of skilled artisans."]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",5]],"s":["Truckers spend so much because the truck is their home and their pride for long periods; decoration expresses their personality, beliefs and love for their family and region, and a beautiful truck attracts more business and respect. It is also believed to bring good luck and protection."]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",6]],"s":["In the 1940s trucks were decorated simply by the companies that built the bodies; gradually painters added floral designs and pictures. By the 1950s and 1960s, with the Bedford trucks, decoration became more elaborate with paintings, calligraphy, mirrors and metal work, and by the 1970s it had become a fully developed popular art form with competing workshops."]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",7]],"s":["They are nostalgic because the old Bedford trucks were the first trucks on which truck art really flourished; they were strong, beautiful and easy to decorate, and they remind the drivers of the golden age of truck art."]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-comprehension-a",8]],"s":["Truck art has reached 'some kind of apogee' (the highest point) because it is now extremely elaborate and colourful, combining painting, calligraphy, mirror-work, steel and plastic decoration; it is famous all over the world, displayed in exhibitions and copied on objects, and has become a symbol of Pakistani popular culture."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u04-exercises-a",1]],"s":["Sample composition — The Need of the Smart Phone: Today the smart phone has become a basic need rather than a luxury. First of all, it keeps us connected with family and friends through calls, messages and video chats. Moreover, it is a powerful tool for learning: students can search information, read e-books and attend online classes. In addition, it makes daily life easier — banking, shopping, navigation and booking tickets are only a touch away. However, it must be used wisely, because excessive use wastes time and harms health. Therefore, if used with balance, the smart phone is a blessing of modern technology. (Transitional devices: first of all, moreover, in addition, however, therefore.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u04-exercises-a",3],["eng12-u04-exercises-a",4]],"s":["Pair activity guide: each student writes three similarities (e.g., same school, love of cricket, same favourite subject) and three differences (e.g., one likes reading, the other sports) about himself and his partner, then they compare and discuss which guesses were right or wrong, using comparison and contrast expressions (both, similarly, whereas, unlike)."]}]}},"5":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u05-vocab-a",1],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",2],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",3],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",4],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",5],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",6],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",7],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",8],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",9],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",10],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",11],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",12]],"d":"eng12-u05-dict","s":["Bike /baɪk/; Bus /bʌs/; Car /kɑ:(r)/; Tire /'taɪə(r)/; Ticket /'tɪkɪt/; Truck /trʌk/; Speed /spi:d/; Patrol /pə'trəʊl/; Road /rəʊd/; Garage /'gærɑ:ʒ/ (or /'gærɪdʒ/)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-vocab-a",14],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",15],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",16],["eng12-u05-vocab-a",17]],"d":"eng12-u05-stress-activity","s":["1. beautiful — 1st syllable (BEAU-ti-ful)","2. between — 2nd (be-TWEEN)","3. management — 1st (MAN-age-ment)","4. campaign — 2nd (cam-PAIGN)","5. financial — 2nd (fi-NAN-cial)","6. candidate — 1st (CAN-di-date)","7. certainly — 1st (CER-tain-ly)","8. material — 2nd (ma-TE-ri-al)","9. newspaper — 1st (NEWS-pa-per)","10. character — 1st (CHAR-ac-ter)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-vocab-b",0]],"d":"eng12-u05-roots","s":["1. mountain — mount","2. rescued — rescue","3. naturalist — nature (natural)","4. stormy — storm","5. loaner — loan","6. kindness — kind","7. answered — answer","8. respectful — respect","9. completed — complete","10. misspell — spell","11. migration — migrate","12. actor — act","13. champion — champ / champion (root word: champion; base 'camp' in Latin)","14. nervous — nerve","15. servant — serve"]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-vocab-b",1]],"s":["Heading for the parsing activities (a) and (b) below — see their answers."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",17]],"s":["1. house (n), lived (v), two (adj), men (n), In (prep)","2. look (v), aged (adj), woman (n), came (v), by (adv)","3. colour (n), cap (n), different (adj), quite (adv), of/from (prep)","4. Now (adv), saw (v), dream (n), spoke (v), many/good (adj), words (n), in (prep)","5. most (adv), cruel (adj), dog (n), was (v), thereabout (adv)","6. must clear up (v), misunderstanding (n), this (adj), between (prep)","7. was (v), fine/green/clean (adj), jacket (n)","8. Nowadays/usually (adv), people (n), talk/meet (v), dearness/streets (n), about/in (prep)","9. saw/spoke (v), dream/father/words (n), many/good (adj), in (prep)","10. circumstances/problem (n), current/kind (adj), think/is/help (v), very (adv), In/of/with (prep)","11. Jack/nose/sunblock (n), covered (v), small (adj), in (prep)","12. threw/could (v), frisbee (n), round (adj), far (adv), as (adv/prep)","13. family/house/vacation (n), rented (v), beach/summer (adj use of nouns), for (prep)","14. sunset/touch/afternoon (n), beautiful/perfect/summer (adj), added (v), to (prep)","15. volleyball/summer (n), is (v), popular (adj), very (adv), during (prep)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",23],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",29],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",30],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",31],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",32],["eng12-u05-vocab-b",33]],"s":["1. thought — verb","2. now — adverb","3. you — pronoun","4. gives — verb","5. best — adjective","6. bought — verb","7. lovely — adjective","8. factory — noun","9. stupid — noun (used here as a noun: a stupid person)","10. under — preposition","11. white — adjective","12. quickly — adverb","13. to — preposition (part of the infinitive 'to buy')","14. because — conjunction","15. It — pronoun"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",2]],"s":["The main theme is self-sacrifice and the power of hope. The will to live can save a person, and true art and love can give that hope — old Behrman sacrifices his life to paint a leaf that saves Johnsy."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",3]],"s":["Sue and Johnsy were young artists who met at a restaurant in Greenwich Village; they found that their tastes in art, food and fashion were so similar that they decided to share a studio apartment."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",4]],"s":["The doctor means that medicine alone cannot cure Johnsy; her recovery depends on her own will to live. Because she has lost hope and believes she will die, her chances are very poor unless she regains interest in life."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",5]],"s":["She kept asking Sue to open the curtains so that she could watch the ivy vine on the wall outside and count its falling leaves; she believed that she would die when the last leaf fell."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",6]],"s":["Her life was saved by the 'last leaf': old Behrman secretly painted a leaf on the wall during a stormy night. Seeing that the leaf did not fall, Johnsy regained hope and the will to live, and she recovered."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",7]],"s":["Seeing the last leaf still clinging to the vine after two stormy nights changed her views. She felt ashamed of her wish to die and said that something had made the leaf stay to show her how wicked she had been. We know it because she asked for soup, a mirror and said she hoped to paint the Bay of Naples one day."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",8]],"s":["Behrman was an old, unsuccessful painter who lived below the girls. He drank too much and was rough in manner, but he had a kind heart and was protective of the young artists. He always dreamed of painting a masterpiece; in the end he painted the last leaf on the wall in a cold storm, caught pneumonia and died — the leaf was his masterpiece."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",9]],"s":["Two young artists, Sue and Johnsy, share a studio. Johnsy falls ill with pneumonia and loses the will to live, believing she will die when the last ivy leaf falls. Sue tells old Behrman, who is moved. During a stormy night he paints a leaf on the wall. The leaf survives, Johnsy regains hope and recovers, but Behrman dies of pneumonia caught that night."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",10]],"s":["We learn that hope and the will to live are very powerful; we should never give up. The story also teaches selfless love and sacrifice — true greatness lies in helping others, as Behrman did."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",11]],"s":["Possible alternate ending: Behrman paints the leaf but is found the next morning shivering and ill. Sue and Johnsy, now recovering, look after him day and night until he too gets well. When the spring comes, the three artists exhibit Behrman's 'Last Leaf' and his long-awaited masterpiece is admired by everyone. (Any logical ending is accepted.)"]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",13]],"s":["Sample summary — The Last Leaf: Beginning — Sue and Johnsy, two young artists, share a studio in Greenwich Village. In November pneumonia strikes and Johnsy falls seriously ill. Middle — She loses all hope and counts the leaves of an old ivy vine outside her window, convinced that she will die when the last leaf falls. Sue tells their neighbour Behrman, an old painter who has never painted his masterpiece. That night a cold storm blows, but in the morning one leaf still clings to the vine, and it stays the next day too. End — Johnsy feels ashamed of her despair, regains the will to live and recovers. Then Sue learns that Behrman has died of pneumonia: he had painted the last leaf on the wall in the storm — his true masterpiece."]},{"q":[["eng12-u05-glossary-questions",15]],"s":["Speaking activity guide: each student stands in turn and says three or four sincere compliments about the desk fellow, e.g., \"Ali is always punctual\", \"He helps others in mathematics\", \"His handwriting is neat\", \"He is very polite\". Only positive words are allowed; the class can note new adjectives of praise used."]}]}},"6":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",15]],"d":"eng12-u06-dict-a","s":["Stage /steɪdʒ/; Infant /'ɪnfənt/; Nurse /nɜ:s/; Satchel /'sætʃəl/; Snail /sneɪl/; Lover /'lʌvə/.","Part B words: Quarrel /'kwɒrəl/; Bubble /'bʌbl/; Teeth /ti:θ/; Pipe /paɪp/."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-vocab-a",0]],"d":"eng12-u06-thesaurus","n":"The book prints \"apparant\" and \"lesson, decrease\" (for apparent and lessen).","s":["select — choose; raise — lift; accurate — correct, right; able — capable, qualified; considerate — thoughtful, caring; divide — separate, split; apparent — obvious; exceptional — remarkable, outstanding; busy — occupied, active; brave — courageous, bold; clarify — explain, simplify; aid — help, assist; diminish — lessen, decrease; convenient — handy, useful; beneficial — helpful, useful; approve — accept, endorse; conscious — aware; brief — short; authentic — real, genuine; average — normal, ordinary."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-vocab-a",1],["eng12-u06-vocab-a",2]],"d":"eng12-u06-latin","n":"The rule table prints \"-urn\" for the Latin ending -um (medium → media).","s":["alumna — alumnae; analysis — analyses; synopsis — synopses; cactus — cacti; crisis — crises; cranium — crania; curriculum — curricula; datum — data; focus — foci; formula — formulae; fungus — fungi; larva — larvae; nucleus — nuclei; referendum — referenda; syllabus — syllabi."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",23],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",29],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",30],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",31],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",32],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",33],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",34],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",35],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",36],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",37],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",38],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",39]],"s":["Falling intonation (↘) — exclamations and statements: 1. You already! 2. You shouldn't have. 3. It's unbelievable! 4. That's not very nice! 5. I don't think I will. 8. You must be joking! 10. Well, I never!","Falling intonation — wh-questions: 6. How could you? 7. Why should I tell you? 9. So what? 12. How do you do? 13. How long have you been building flutes?","11. You know best, don't you? — falling on the tag (the speaker expects agreement).","Rising intonation (↗) — yes/no questions: 14. Can I see one of your flutes? 15. Is this your flute?"]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-vocab-b",41],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",42],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",43],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",44],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",45],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",46],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",47],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",48],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",49],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",50],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",51],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",52],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",53],["eng12-u06-vocab-b",54]],"s":["1. The wicked man; poor people","2. having to punish his servants","3. to answer my question","4. The boy; stealing the money","5. The accused; to answer the question","6. having to punish my kids","7. to do such a thing","8. To write such a rubbish","9. to get me something","10. to meet him","11. Your doing such a thing","12. such a kind man","13. the back garden"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",1]],"s":["The main theme is the qualities that make a complete and successful man: self-control, patience, honesty, courage, balance in success and failure, perseverance and humility. Kipling advises his son that if he develops these virtues, the world will be his and he will be a true man."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",2]],"s":["Qualities in stanza two: to dream but not be ruled by dreams; to think but not make thoughts his only aim; to meet triumph and disaster calmly and treat both the same (as impostors); to bear his words being twisted by knaves; to see the things he gave his life to broken and rebuild them with worn-out tools."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",3]],"s":["It means that a man should be able to mix with great and powerful people without becoming proud, and still keep his simplicity and sympathy for ordinary people. Success and high company should not make him lose touch with the common man."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",4]],"s":["Triumph and Disaster are personified as two impostors: \"If you can meet with Triumph and Disaster / And treat those two impostors just the same\". The 'Will' that says \"Hold on!\" to the heart and nerves is also personified."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",5]],"s":["The title \"If\" is significant because the whole poem is a series of conditions: each 'if' states a quality a man must have. Only if all these conditions are fulfilled will the son become a true man — the title sums up this conditional advice."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",6]],"s":["\"If you can fill the unforgiving minute with sixty seconds' worth of distance run\" — time is precious and passes without mercy; we should use every second of every minute fully and productively, because lost time never comes back."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",7]],"s":["After listing all the qualities, the father says that if the son has them, the earth and everything in it will be his, and — what is even greater than that — he will become a true man: mature, complete and noble in character."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",8]],"s":["The rhyme scheme is ABAB (alternate rhyme) in each quatrain, e.g., 'you / you' and 'you / too' — giving four eight-line stanzas of the pattern ABAB CDCD."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",9]],"s":["Other qualities of a successful man: faith in Allah and honesty, hard work and discipline, good manners and respect for others, a thirst for knowledge, sense of responsibility, time management, kindness and helpfulness, leadership and the ability to work in a team."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",11]],"s":["Sample essay — A Person with the Qualities of 'If': My grandfather is the person who reminds me of Kipling's poem. He remained calm in success and failure: when his business suffered a heavy loss, he did not complain but started again with patience. He never told lies and never hated those who wronged him. Though he met officers and important people, he never lost his common touch and treated the poor with kindness. He used every minute of his day well — prayer, work, reading and helping neighbours. These qualities made him a true man respected by all."]},{"q":[["eng12-u06-comprehension",13]],"s":["Role-play guide: 'fathers' advise 'sons' on behaviour in different situations — e.g., Son: \"How should I behave if I fail an exam?\" Father: \"Do not lose heart; learn from mistakes and work harder.\" Son: \"What if people blame me unfairly?\" Father: \"Stay patient and truthful; don't answer hatred with hatred.\" Students use the advice of the poem 'If' in their dialogue."]}]}},"7":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u07-vocab-a",1]],"d":"eng12-u07-idioms","s":["To brighten up — to become more cheerful; To take a shine to — to develop a liking for; To make hay when the sun shines — to make the most of an opportunity while it lasts; Come rain or shine — whatever the weather/situation; To chase rainbows — to pursue something impossible/impractical; To save for a rainy day — to put something aside for a future time of need; To wait for a raindrop in the drought — to wait/hope for something which has little chance of happening.","Right as rain — fit and healthy; To take a rain check — to refuse an offer while showing willingness to accept it later; Into each life some rain must fall — everyone must experience difficulties at some time; To rain on someone's parade — to spoil someone's plans; It never rains but it pours — misfortunes come all at once; Raining cats and dogs — raining heavily; Dry spell — a period of less success than usual; Windfall — a large amount of money unexpectedly received.","To shoot the breeze — to have a casual conversation; To get wind of something — to hear a rumour about something; To know which way the wind blows — to understand changing circumstances and anticipate what will happen; To sail close to the wind — to verge on the limit of what is acceptable; To throw caution to the wind — to take a great risk; Cloud/storm on the horizon — an approaching problem; Every dark cloud has a silver lining — every difficult situation has a hopeful aspect; Cloud nine — a state of great happiness; To have your head in the clouds — to have unrealistic or fanciful ideas; \"I don't have the foggiest\" — I have no idea."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-vocab-a",5]],"d":"eng12-u07-prefixes","s":["dissatisfied (not satisfied); misspell (spell wrongly); unacceptable (not acceptable); re-election (election again) / pre-election (before the election).","interrelated (related to each other); prepay (pay before) / repay (pay back); nonsense (no sense); subscript (written under) / superscript (written above); submerge (put under water); antibacterial (acting against bacteria)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",12]],"n":"Item 6 prints \"envoy\" for envy.","s":["1. Liaqat Ali Khan, the first Prime Minister of Pakistan, was assassinated in 1951.","2. The annual flow of the Indus, the longest river of Pakistan, is about 243 cubic kilometres.","3. Ustad Ahmad Lahori, the chief architect of the Taj Mahal in Agra, India, hailed from Lahore.","4. John Kennedy, a popular US President, was known for his eloquent and inspirational speeches.","5. The Islamic Republic of Iran, our neighbouring country, was the first country to recognise Pakistan.","6. My uncle's car, a sporty red convertible with bucket seats, is the envy of my friends.","7. The chief surgeon, an expert in organ transplant procedures, took his daughter on a hospital tour.","8. The problem, that you did not come on time, delayed the entire program.","9. The worry, who we should invite, troubles me too much.","10. Hamad, my younger brother, is very dear to me."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",23],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u07-vocab-b",29]],"n":"Only part of the clause list is legible in the scan; items not covered by it are completed with suitable noun clauses.","s":["1. The prize will be given to whoever wins the race / whichever charity you choose.","2. Do you know what time it is?","3. I don't understand what you are talking about.","4. That he couldn't speak fluently made his friends angry.","5. What Hurain wrote astonished her teachers.","6. What he said was not very agreeable.","7. The food inspector checked where they live now / what they were selling.","8. She did not know that you were coming with us.","9. His problem was that he couldn't speak fluently.","10. Hammad's great achievement at school was when he became the class proctor.","11. The teacher is not responsible for what you do outside the school.","12. The money will go to whichever charity you choose.","13. It's very disappointing that you were not selected.","14. They are perfectly happy where they live now.","15. Umair runs so fast that nobody can catch him."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",1]],"s":["Earth's atmosphere blurs, absorbs and distorts light from space and blocks ultraviolet and infrared rays. A telescope above the atmosphere gets sharp, clear images and can see much fainter and more distant objects, so cosmologists placed Hubble in space."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",2]],"s":["Light takes time to travel; when we look at a distant star we see the light that left it long ago — thousands or even billions of years — so we are seeing the star as it was in the past, not as it is now. Looking at stars is like looking into history."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",3]],"s":["Because galaxies are mostly empty space: the distances between stars are so huge that when the Milky Way and Andromeda collide, the stars will pass by each other without crashing; the Sun and Earth will only drift to a new place in the merged galaxy, so life on Earth will not be disturbed."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",4]],"s":["The Hubble Deep Field is an image made by pointing Hubble at a tiny, apparently empty patch of sky for many days. It revealed thousands of very distant galaxies of different shapes and ages, showing the universe as it was billions of years ago."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",5]],"s":["In star nurseries (huge clouds of gas and dust), gravity pulls the gas together into dense clumps; as they shrink they heat up and form protostars. When the core becomes hot enough, nuclear fusion ignites and a new star is born, while the leftover material may form planets."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",6]],"s":["When an exoplanet passes in front of its star (transit), a little starlight passes through the planet's atmosphere. Each gas absorbs particular colours (wavelengths) of light, leaving a unique 'fingerprint' in the spectrum; by spectroscopy scientists read these fingerprints to know which gases (water vapour, methane, etc.) are present."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",7]],"s":["In astronomy the light we see is old and may be bent, dimmed or reddened on its way, and objects may have changed or no longer exist. Gravity can bend light (gravitational lensing), making objects appear in different places or shapes — so what we see is not always the true present state of the object."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u07-comprehension",9],["eng12-u07-comprehension",10],["eng12-u07-comprehension",11],["eng12-u07-comprehension",12],["eng12-u07-comprehension",13],["eng12-u07-writing-b",0],["eng12-u07-writing-b",1],["eng12-u07-writing-b",2],["eng12-u07-writing-b",3],["eng12-u07-writing-b",4],["eng12-u07-writing-b",5],["eng12-u07-writing-b",6],["eng12-u07-writing-b",7],["eng12-u07-writing-b",8],["eng12-u07-writing-b",9],["eng12-u07-writing-b",10],["eng12-u07-writing-b",11],["eng12-u07-writing-b",12],["eng12-u07-writing-b",13],["eng12-u07-writing-b",14],["eng12-u07-writing-b",15],["eng12-u07-writing-b",16]],"s":["Sample essay — Old Means of Communication vs Modern Means of Communication (point-by-point): Introduction: Communication has always been a human need, but its means have changed greatly. Point 1 — Speed: In the past messages were carried by messengers, pigeons or letters that took days or weeks; whereas today e-mail, mobile phones and social media deliver messages in seconds. Point 2 — Reach: Old means were limited to nearby places; in contrast, modern means connect the whole world instantly. Point 3 — Cost and ease: Sending a messenger or letter was slow and costly; similarly, telegrams were expensive, but now messages are almost free and easy. Point 4 — Personal touch: However, old letters were personal and preserved as memories, while modern messages are quick but often casual. Conclusion: Modern means are faster and more convenient, yet we should keep the warmth and care of the old ways."]},{"q":[["eng12-u07-writing-b",18]],"s":["Guessing game guide: one student gets a secret word (e.g., 'sun') and explains it without saying it — \"It rises in the east, gives us light and heat, and plants need it to make food.\" Others listen carefully and guess. The activity practises descriptive speaking and listening."]}]}},"8":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",15],["eng12-u08-comprehension",16],["eng12-u08-comprehension",17],["eng12-u08-comprehension",18],["eng12-u08-comprehension",19],["eng12-u08-comprehension",20],["eng12-u08-comprehension",21],["eng12-u08-comprehension",22],["eng12-u08-comprehension",23],["eng12-u08-comprehension",24],["eng12-u08-comprehension",25],["eng12-u08-comprehension",26],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",6]],"d":"eng12-u08-econ-box","s":["A. 1. inflation; 2. recession; 3. consumer; 4. economics; 5. deficit; 6. budget; 7. credit; 8. capital; 9. fiscal; 10. investment; 11. currency; 12. cost; 13. stock; 14. finance; 15. deposit; 16. bankrupt.","B. Pronunciation practice: say the CAPITAL syllable louder and longer — cruSADE, arCADE, aGREE, guaranTEE, sightSEER, puppeTEER, SiamESE, JapanESE, cassETTE, unIQUE, physIQUE, balLOON, afterNOON, carTOON, iCONic, GRAPHic, hyperTENsion, teleVIsion, nuTRItion, reveLAtion, deMOcracy, geOGraphy, ALlergy (oral practice — no written answer)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u08-vocab-b",20]],"s":["1. discomfort","2. mismatched","3. unglamorous","4. anti-aging","5. substandard","6. supernatural","7. relocate","8. interact","9. precautions","10. non-combatant"]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-vocab-b",22]],"d":"eng12-u08-zero-cond","s":["1. If I am late for class, my teacher gets angry.","2. When he stays up very late, he is very tired the next morning.","3. People get hungry if they don't eat.","4. If you study hard, you get good grades in school.","5. When she watches a movie, she likes to eat popcorn.","6. When I cross the street, I always look left and right.","7. I can't do my homework if I don't have my glasses.","8. He always smiles when he is happy.","9. If I miss the bus, I take a taxi to work.","10. When you make lots of noise, the librarian gets angry.","11. Tea tastes sweet if you add some sugar.","12. You should eat less if you want to lose weight.","13. I always take my umbrella when it rains.","14. When I'm sad, I watch a funny movie.","15. When he cleans the house, he listens to music."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",1]],"s":["The Quraish army of about three thousand men marched to Madinah under Abu Sufyan; it included about seven hundred men in armour, two hundred horsemen and three thousand camels, with women who sang war songs to encourage the soldiers."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",2]],"s":["The Holy Prophet (ﷺ) suggested staying inside Madinah and defending it from within, because the city could be defended easily from its narrow streets and houses, the Muslims were far fewer than the enemy, and fighting in the open would be risky."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",3]],"s":["The young Companions, especially those who had missed Badr, were full of zeal and wanted to go out of the city and meet the enemy in the open battlefield; they felt that staying inside would look like weakness and cowardice."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",4]],"s":["He preferred the opinion of the youth because the majority favoured it and Islam teaches consultation (shura); he respected their enthusiasm and courage. Once the decision was made he put on his armour and did not go back on it."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",5]],"s":["At first the Muslims were winning and the Quraish fled. Seeing this, most of the fifty archers placed on the hill (Jabal al-Rumah) left their post to collect the spoils, against the Prophet's (ﷺ) order. Khalid bin Walid, then a commander of the Quraish cavalry, attacked from behind through the gap, and the battle turned against the Muslims."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",6]],"s":["When the infidels spread the false rumour that the Holy Prophet (ﷺ) had been martyred, many Muslims lost heart and some left the field, while others fought on bravely. When the Companions saw him alive, they gathered around him, protected him and took him to a safe place on the mountain; the Muslims regrouped and the Quraish withdrew."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",7]],"s":["The lesson is that obedience to the leader and discipline are essential for success; greed for worldly gain leads to disaster. It also teaches patience in hardship, unity, steadfastness and trust in Allah."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",8]],"s":["If the archers had not left the hill, Khalid's cavalry could not have attacked from behind; the Muslims would have completed their victory and the Quraish would have suffered a defeat like that of Badr."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",9]],"s":["At Badr (2 AH) 313 Muslims defeated a much larger Quraish army because of faith, obedience and Allah's help. Uhud (3 AH) was the Quraish's revenge for Badr; the Muslims began winning but suffered losses because of disobedience. Together they show that success comes with faith and obedience, and loss follows disobedience."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",11]],"s":["Sample narration — The Battle of Uhud: Beginning — To take revenge for Badr, the Quraish marched on Madinah in 3 AH with about three thousand men. The Holy Prophet (ﷺ) consulted his Companions and, on the advice of the young men, went out to meet them near Mount Uhud with about seven hundred men after the hypocrites withdrew. He placed fifty archers on a hill with strict orders not to leave it. Middle — The Muslims attacked bravely and the Quraish fled. Seeing the spoils, most archers left the hill. Khalid bin Walid struck from behind; Hazrat Hamzah was martyred; the Prophet (ﷺ) was wounded and a rumour of his martyrdom spread. End — The Companions gathered round him and moved to the mountain; the Quraish went back without taking Madinah. The battle taught the Muslims the value of obedience and discipline."]},{"q":[["eng12-u08-comprehension",13]],"s":["Presentation guide: students read their narration of the Battle of Uhud to the class, using a clear beginning, middle and end, proper pronunciation and expression; classmates give feedback on organisation and language."]}]}},"9":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",18],["eng12-u09-comprehension",19],["eng12-u09-comprehension",20],["eng12-u09-comprehension",21],["eng12-u09-comprehension",22],["eng12-u09-comprehension",23],["eng12-u09-comprehension",24],["eng12-u09-comprehension",25],["eng12-u09-comprehension",26],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",7]],"d":"eng12-u09-weather-box","n":"Several weather words fit some blanks; the most natural choice is given first.","s":["1. breezy","2. bright (clear)","3. dry / clear","4. cloudy","5. damp (wet)","6. showery","7. dry","8. dry","9. stormy","10. hazy / foggy","11. foggy (rainy)","12. rainy / showery","13. snowy","14. dull","15. wet / snowy"]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",20]],"d":"eng12-u09-shades","s":["i. Same shade of meaning: big – large – enormous; shout – yell – bellow; nibble – eat – gobble; scared – frightened – terrified; toss – throw – slam; misting – sprinkling – pouring.","ii. Stronger word: 1. screamed; 2. freezing; 3. furious; 4. pouring; 5. gigantic.","iii. Weaker word: 1. whispered; 2. cool; 3. cross; 4. sprinkling; 5. big."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",23],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",29],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",30],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",31],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",32],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",33],["eng12-u09-vocab-b",34],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",0],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",1],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",2],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",3],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",4],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",5],["eng12-u09-vocab-c",6]],"s":["1. go, will go","2. get, will be","3. don't see, will see","4. comes, will be","5. wait, will be","6. go, will go","7. doesn't improve, won't have","8. don't go, will be","9. eat, will feel","10. don't want, will cook","11. will come, want","12. will go, are","13. will stay, gets","14. won't get, doesn't pass","15. will buy, have","16. will cook, go","17. will go, have","18. will be, don't hurry","19. will take, rains","20. won't go, don't come"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",1]],"s":["The theme is the comparison between a father's love for his child and God's mercy for mankind. Just as the father forgives his son after seeing his childish toys, God will forgive human beings who, like children, are busy with worldly 'toys' and fail to understand His commands."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",2]],"s":["The poet's little son disobeyed him for the seventh time, so the poet struck him and sent him to bed without a kiss. Later, feeling sorry, he went to the child's room and found him asleep with tear-stained eyes. Beside the bed the boy had placed his little toys — a box of counters, a red-veined stone, a piece of glass, shells, bluebells and coins — to comfort himself. The father wept and prayed that God would be merciful to people as he had now felt for his son."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",3]],"s":["The poem is written in irregular (free) verse — an ode-like style with lines of varying length and an irregular rhyme scheme, close to the rhythm of natural speech."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",4]],"s":["Yes. The irregular lines and natural speech rhythm suit the personal, emotional and confessional mood; they express the father's changing feelings — anger, regret, tenderness and prayer — sincerely and naturally."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",5]],"s":["The toys symbolise the worldly things that human beings value and play with — wealth, power and pleasures — which are as trivial in God's sight as the child's toys are to an adult. They also show the child's innocence and the comfort we seek in small things."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",6]],"s":["The poet compares himself to God: as he, a father, forgave his son after seeing his innocence and sorrow, he hopes that God, who is far more fatherly and merciful than he, will forgive human beings for their childish behaviour and sins on the Day of Judgement."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",7]],"s":["The tone is sad, regretful and tender, ending in a prayerful, hopeful note. Yes, it matches the theme: the regret of the father leads to his realisation of God's mercy, so the gentle, sorrowful tone fits the message of love and forgiveness."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",8]],"s":["The poem evokes feelings of sympathy for the innocent child, regret for harsh treatment, tenderness and love between parent and child, and hope and gratitude for the infinite mercy of Allah. It teaches us to be gentle and forgiving."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",10],["eng12-u09-comprehension",11],["eng12-u09-comprehension",12],["eng12-u09-comprehension",13],["eng12-u09-comprehension",14]],"s":["Paraphrase: Then (on the Day of Judgement), O God, You — who are no less fatherly than I, Your creature whom You have made from clay — will give up Your anger and say, \"I will forgive them for their childish behaviour.\" The poet hopes that God, like a loving father, will pity and forgive human beings for their foolishness just as he forgave his son."]},{"q":[["eng12-u09-comprehension",16]],"s":["Oral recount guide: students share a real experience — e.g., \"Once my father scolded me for breaking a glass. I cried and went to my room. Later he came, sat beside me and explained gently. I felt loved and learned to be careful.\" They should describe what happened, how they felt and what they learned."]}]}},"10":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",11]],"d":"eng12-u10-prefix-bank","n":"Some grid words accept more than one prefix; one or two common answers are given.","s":["Row 1: prefix / suffix (re-fix); nonsense; unhappy; displace / replace; unusual; important.","Row 2: remind; expert; disrespect; withdraw; illogical; retrieve.","Row 3: unequal; illegal; nonfiction; invisible; display / replay; excellent.","Row 4: disprove / improve; defrost; exceed / proceed; preview / review; decode; reflex.","Row 5: unlucky; mislead; non-fat; unfair; disgust; withhold.","Sample sentences: The thief tried to withhold the truth. He tried to mislead the police. The window was defrosted in the morning."]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",12],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",13],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",14],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",15],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",16],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",17],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",18],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",19],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",20],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",21],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",22],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",23],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",24],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",25]],"s":["1. will give","2. will go","3. snows","4. won't succeed","5. bring","6. won't let","7. gets","8. read","9. go, will visit","10. does, will go","11. won't eat, eats","12. will drive, ask","13. will phone, wants"]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",26],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",27],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",28],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",29],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",30],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",31],["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",32]],"s":["1. comes","2. have","3. hurry","4. will take","5. is","6. sees"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",1]],"s":["I agree with the writer: gender inequality is rooted in social customs and attitudes rather than in religion; denying women education, health care and economic opportunities harms not only women but the whole society's progress. (Students may give their own reasoned view.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",2]],"s":["Many Pakistani women, especially in rural areas, lack proper health care: high maternal and infant mortality, malnutrition and anaemia, early marriages, lack of trained midwives and lady doctors, and neglect of girls' health compared with boys. Social restrictions and poverty also keep them from reaching hospitals."]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",3]],"s":["Factors include low literacy and lack of skills, early marriage, social customs that limit women's work outside the home, denial of inheritance and property rights, unequal wages, lack of safe transport and workplaces, and limited access to loans and business opportunities."]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",4]],"s":["Islam gives women dignity and equal human worth: \"O mankind! We created you from a single male and female\" (49:13); men and women get equal reward for good deeds (4:124). Women have rights of education, property, inheritance (4:7), mahr, consent in marriage and kind treatment — the Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"The best of you are those who are best to their wives\" and \"Seeking knowledge is obligatory on every Muslim.\""]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",5]],"s":["Example: in my area girls are often withdrawn from school after the primary level while boys continue to college; or at home sons get better food and freedom than daughters. (Students describe an incident they have observed.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",6]],"s":["The motive is to make readers aware of gender inequality and its harmful effects on women and society, and to urge people and the government to give women their rights in education, health and economic life in the light of Islam."]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-comprehension",7]],"s":["Suggestions: free and compulsory education for girls; awareness campaigns about women's Islamic rights; strict implementation of laws on inheritance, harassment and early marriage; better health facilities and lady health workers; vocational training and jobs for women; equal pay; and changing attitudes through media and religious scholars."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",7]],"s":["Sample argumentative essay — The bread earner has always got a stronger social status: Introduction — In most societies the person who earns money for the family enjoys more respect and authority. Arguments — First, money gives power to make decisions, so the earner controls the family budget. Second, society values paid work more than unpaid work such as housework and child care. Third, economic independence gives confidence and freedom. Counter-argument — However, Islam and good sense teach that status should depend on character and contribution, not income; the mother's role in raising children is as valuable as earning. Conclusion — It is true that the bread earner has usually enjoyed a stronger status, but a just society should honour every member's contribution and give women equal opportunities to earn."]},{"q":[["eng12-u10-writing-vocab",9]],"s":["Debate guide — For women equality: equal rights in Islam, education, economic participation and national progress, examples of successful women. Against (as debate practice): traditional roles, family responsibilities, cultural values. Each speaker gets two minutes; the teacher concludes with the Islamic view of justice and dignity for women."]}]}},"11":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u11-vocab-a",22],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",23],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",24],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",25],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",26],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",27],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",28],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",29],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",30],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",31]],"s":["1. Nearby","2. Similarly","3. Even if","4. Ultimately","5. In the meantime","6. Consequently","7. Furthermore","8. In other words"]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-vocab-a",32],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",33],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",34],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",35],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",36],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",37],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",38],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",39],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",40],["eng12-u11-vocab-a",41],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",0]],"n":"Sentence 2 needs a contrast (\"however\"); \"although\" from the box is used as the nearest choice.","s":["1. likewise","2. although (however)","3. therefore","4. for example / likewise","5. meanwhile","6. consequently","7. for example","8. therefore / consequently"]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u11-vocab-b",21]],"s":["1. were, would get","2. were, would travel","3. weren't, would be","4. had, would buy","5. weren't, would be","6. won, would travel","7. had, would be","8. spoke, would have","9. lived, would speak","10. passed, would be","11. would be, had","12. would buy, decided","13. would have, didn't buy","14. would come, had","15. would call, knew","16. would go, liked","17. would pass, studied","18. would marry, were","19. would never be, bought","20. would lose, ate"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",1]],"s":["Jahangir Khan was a weak and sickly child and had hernia operations, so doctors advised him to avoid physical activity. But squash was in his blood — his father Roshan Khan and brother Torsam were champions — and he had a strong will to play and succeed, so he ignored the advice and kept practising."]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",2]],"s":["He followed a very hard training routine: long runs (about nine miles a day), sprints, rope skipping, weight training and yoga, hours of court practice every day, a disciplined diet and early sleep — all under the guidance of his father and cousin Rehmat Khan."]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",3]],"s":["He won the World Amateur Championship at fifteen, became the youngest World Open champion at seventeen (1981), won the World Open six times and the British Open a record ten times in a row (1982–1991), and remained unbeaten for over five years (about 555 matches) — one of the longest winning streaks in sport."]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",4]],"s":["Jansher Khan, another Pakistani squash legend, also won the World Open a record eight times and dominated squash in the 1990s. Like Jahangir he showed great fitness and determination, but Jahangir is remembered for his unbeaten run of five years, while Jansher is known for the most World Open titles. (Students may compare any national sports hero.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",5]],"s":["Examples: 'to have something in one's blood' — to inherit a talent or interest from family; 'to rise to the occasion' — to perform well when needed; 'to make history' — to do something important that will be remembered. (Any three idioms from the text with correct meanings.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",6]],"s":["His life teaches that weakness can be overcome by determination, hard work and discipline; that respect for elders and guidance, humility in victory and self-belief lead to greatness; and that a person can bring honour to his country through dedication."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",8]],"s":["Sample essay — The Present State of Squash in Pakistan: Pakistan once ruled the world of squash — Hashim Khan, Roshan Khan, Jahangir Khan and Jansher Khan won the British and World Opens for decades. Today, however, Pakistan has no player in the world's top rankings. Reasons include lack of modern facilities and coaching, few courts outside a few cities, poor sponsorship, the security situation that stopped international events, and the popularity of cricket. To revive squash, the government and federation should build courts in schools, hire foreign coaches, organise junior tournaments and give scholarships to young talent. With proper planning Pakistan can regain its lost glory."]},{"q":[["eng12-u11-comprehension",10]],"s":["Group sharing guide: students who like the same game sit together and each gives reasons — e.g., cricket: teamwork and national popularity; squash: fitness and speed; football: global appeal; hockey: our national game — then one member reports the group's main reasons to the class."]}]}},"12":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u12-vocab-a",3]],"d":"eng12-u12-word-mcq","s":["Mewling — d. crying","Satchel — b. bag for books","Woeful — a. sad","Pard — c. leopard","Capon — b. fat chicken","Saws — a. sayings","Instances — b. judgements (examples/cases)","Pantaloon — a. pants (a foolish old man in loose trousers)","Shank — d. legs","Treble — c. high-pitched sound","Oblivion — d. forgetfulness","Sans — c. without"]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-vocab-a",4],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",5],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",6],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",7],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",8],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",9],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",10],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",11],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",12],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",13],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",14],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",15],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",16],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",17],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",18],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",19],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",20],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",21],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",22],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",23],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",24],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",25],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",26],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",27],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",28],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",29],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",30],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",31],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",32],["eng12-u12-vocab-a",33],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",14]],"n":"Item 5 is missing from the printed exercise (numbering jumps from 4 to 6).","s":["1. were, would be","2. had, would give","3. won, would buy","4. had, would love","6. wouldn't drive, were","7. were, wouldn't ride","8. told, would be","9. would spend, were","10. would help, knew","11. felt, could","12. went, wouldn't be","13. wouldn't call, didn't want"]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",23],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",29],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",30],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",31],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",32],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",33],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",34],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",35],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",36],["eng12-u12-vocab-b",37]],"s":["1. simple","2. simple","3. simple","4. complex","5. compound","6. simple","7. complex (that-clause understood)","8. compound","9. compound","10. complex","11. complex","12. complex","13. complex","14. compound","15. simple","16. complex","17. simple","18. compound","19. compound","20. complex","21. simple","22. compound"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",1]],"s":["The poem compares the world to a stage and human life to a play: men and women are actors, birth and death are their entrances and exits, and the seven stages of life are the acts of the play."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",2]],"s":["The seven ages are: 1. the infant; 2. the schoolboy; 3. the lover; 4. the soldier; 5. the justice (judge); 6. the old man (the lean and slippered pantaloon); 7. second childishness and mere oblivion (extreme old age)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",3]],"s":["'Oblivion' means a state of complete forgetfulness. In the last stage the old man loses memory and awareness, as well as his teeth, eyes and taste — he is 'sans everything', almost unaware of the world before death."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",4],["eng12-u12-comprehension",5],["eng12-u12-comprehension",6],["eng12-u12-comprehension",7]],"s":["(a) The soldier is ready to risk his life, even facing the cannon's mouth, to win fame and honour. (b) Simile — 'bearded like a pard' (a leopard). (c) 'Bubble reputation' means fame that is short-lived and empty, which bursts like a bubble."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",8]],"s":["'Entrances' and 'exits' stand for birth and death: just as actors come onto and leave the stage, people are born into the world, play their roles and then die."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",9]],"s":["Metaphor — 'All the world's a stage'; simile — 'creeping like snail', 'sighing like furnace', 'bearded like a pard'; personification and imagery — 'the cannon's mouth'; alliteration — 'sans teeth, sans eyes, sans taste, sans everything' (also repetition); irony — 'seeking the bubble reputation'."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",10]],"s":["Sample essay — The Stages of Human Life: Shakespeare shows life as a seven-act play. It begins with the helpless infant crying in the nurse's arms, then the unwilling schoolboy creeping to school. Youth brings the lover sighing over his beloved and the brave soldier seeking fame. In middle age man becomes the wise justice, full of wise sayings. Then comes old age, when his body shrinks and his voice turns childish, and finally second childhood — without teeth, eyes or taste. The poem reminds us that life is short and every stage has its role; we should play our part well."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u12-comprehension",12]],"s":["Summary — 'All the World's a Stage' (Shakespeare): The poet says the whole world is a stage and all people are actors who enter at birth and exit at death, each playing many parts in seven ages. First, the infant cries and vomits in the nurse's arms; then the schoolboy goes unwillingly to school; next, the lover sighs and writes sad poems for his beloved; then the soldier, quick to quarrel, risks his life for short-lived fame; then the justice, well-fed and wise, gives judgements and sayings; in the sixth age the old man becomes thin, wears spectacles and slippers, and his voice turns shrill; finally comes second childhood and forgetfulness, without teeth, eyes, taste or anything."]},{"q":[["eng12-u12-vocab-a",1]],"s":["Role-sharing guide: each student chooses a stage of life (child, student, soldier, judge, old man) and describes its traits — e.g., the student: curious, hopeful but sometimes lazy; the old man: wise but weak and forgetful — and shares observations from people he knows."]}]}},"13":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",11],["eng12-u13-comprehension",12],["eng12-u13-comprehension",13],["eng12-u13-comprehension",14],["eng12-u13-comprehension",15],["eng12-u13-comprehension",16],["eng12-u13-comprehension",17],["eng12-u13-comprehension",18],["eng12-u13-comprehension",19],["eng12-u13-comprehension",20],["eng12-u13-comprehension",21],["eng12-u13-comprehension",22],["eng12-u13-comprehension",23],["eng12-u13-comprehension",24],["eng12-u13-comprehension",25],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",23]],"n":"This activity follows a web glossary of connotations; the book's text keeps phrases like \"as this page points out\".","s":["1. customer, client","2. job, career","3. coworker, colleague","4. Expensive; overpriced; top-of-the-line, premium","5. Ordinary, standard (neutral); run-of-the-mill, common (negative); familiar, well-known (positive)","6. Rich; wealthy; well-off, well-to-do","7. scheme, plot (negative); approach/method, strategy (positive)","8. plant, factory (synonyms); sweatshop (negative); workshop is smaller than a factory; a workshop is a place where things may be manufactured and/or repaired","9. warranty; guarantee","10. ashamed; embarrassed; mortified","11. boss (negative); manager, supervisor; supervisor is in charge of people; manager may have additional responsibilities","12. cost, price (neutral); expense (negative); investment (positive)","13. plenty more positive than sufficient/adequate; adequate = barely enough; sufficient = enough; plenty = much more than needed","14. to fail (negative); to come up short, to miss the mark (more positive)","15. young (neutral), youthful (positive), immature (negative)","16. look / stare at something not moving (look at the screen, stare at me, look at the picture, look at the chart); watch for something moving; glare = look angrily","17. honest (telling the truth); sincere (genuine feelings)","18. stingy, cheap (negative); thrifty, frugal (positive); stingy = not wanting to part with money; cheap = not wanting to part with anything of value","19. unusual (neutral); odd, strange, weird (negative); unique (positive)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",29],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",30],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",31],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",32],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",33],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",34],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",35],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",36],["eng12-u13-vocab-b",37],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",0],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",1],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",2],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",3],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",4],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",5],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",6]],"s":["1. hadn't been, wouldn't have missed","2. had studied, would have passed","3. had arrived, would have seen","4. had gone, wouldn't have woken","5. had become, would have recorded","6. had gone, would have become","7. had been, would have learned","8. had gone, would have studied","9. hadn't gone, wouldn't have met","10. had taken, wouldn't have gone","11. would have been, had stayed","12. would have passed, had studied","13. wouldn't have got, hadn't gone","14. would have been, hadn't taken","15. wouldn't have met, hadn't come","16. would have taken, had had","17. would have called, hadn't forgotten","18. would have come, had been","19. wouldn't have done, had known","20. would have been, had left"]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-vocab-c",7]],"d":"eng12-u13-cond-match","s":["I wouldn't have overslept if my alarm clock had gone off.","If she had gone to bed earlier, she wouldn't have been so tired.","I could have lent you the money if you'd asked.","They wouldn't have found out if you hadn't told them.","You could have been a doctor if you had studied medicine.","If you'd been more organised, you wouldn't have been so stressed at the last minute.","If they'd taken my advice, they wouldn't have had so many problems."]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-vocab-c",8],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",9],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",10],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",11],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",12],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",13],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",14],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",15],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",16],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",17],["eng12-u13-vocab-c",18]],"n":"Sentences 4 and 7 have one subject with two verbs joined by \"and\"; strictly they are simple sentences with a compound predicate.","s":["1. simple","2. simple","3. simple","4. compound (simple with compound predicate)","5. simple","6. simple","7. compound (simple with compound predicate)","8. complex","9. compound","10. complex"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",1]],"s":["Pakistan needs more technical education because its economy needs skilled workers — technicians, engineers and craftsmen — for industry, agriculture and services. General education produces many graduates who cannot find jobs, while technical education gives practical skills, self-employment and higher productivity."]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",2]],"s":["After World War II both countries were ruined, but they invested heavily in technical and vocational training. Skilled workers rebuilt industries, produced high-quality goods (cars, machines, electronics) and exported them, so Japan and Germany quickly became industrial powers."]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",3]],"s":["Linkage is necessary so that training centres teach the skills industries actually need, students get on-the-job training and apprenticeships, industries get trained workers, and the curriculum keeps up with new technology; it reduces unemployment and wastage."]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",4]],"s":["Measures: more polytechnics and vocational institutes in every district; modern equipment and trained teachers; up-to-date curricula linked with industry; apprenticeship schemes; scholarships and loans; respect and good salaries for technical jobs; career counselling; and public-private partnership."]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",5]],"s":["Technical education creates skilled manpower, raises industrial and agricultural production, encourages small businesses and self-employment, reduces unemployment and poverty, improves product quality for export, attracts foreign investment and lets Pakistani workers earn more abroad — all of which strengthen the economy."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",7]],"s":["Sample argumentative essay — Need for Technical Education in Our Country: Opinion — Pakistan's progress depends on technical education. Supporting ideas: (1) Unemployment among general graduates is high, while industries lack skilled workers. (2) Developed nations such as Germany, Japan and China grew through technical skills. (3) Technical education promotes self-employment and small businesses. (4) Skilled workers earn foreign exchange. Counter-view — some think technical jobs have low status; but in reality they offer good income and respect. Conclusion — the government and society should make technical education attractive and accessible."]},{"q":[["eng12-u13-comprehension",9]],"s":["Group discussion points: why youth prefer white-collar jobs (social status, parents' wishes); disadvantages (unemployment, frustration); benefits of technical skills (jobs at home and abroad, own business); suggestions to change the trend (career counselling, awareness, better pay, success stories of skilled workers)."]}]}},"14":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u14-vocab",3],["eng12-u14-vocab",4],["eng12-u14-vocab",5],["eng12-u14-vocab",6],["eng12-u14-vocab",7],["eng12-u14-vocab",8],["eng12-u14-vocab",9],["eng12-u14-vocab",10],["eng12-u14-vocab",11],["eng12-u14-vocab",12],["eng12-u14-vocab",13]],"s":["1. strength","2. poverty","3. Cruelty","4. pilgrimage","5. pleasure","6. Childhood","7. Wastage (Waste)","8. safety","9. darkness","10. wickedness"]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-vocab",14],["eng12-u14-vocab",15],["eng12-u14-vocab",16],["eng12-u14-vocab",17],["eng12-u14-vocab",18],["eng12-u14-vocab",19],["eng12-u14-vocab",20],["eng12-u14-vocab",21],["eng12-u14-vocab",22],["eng12-u14-vocab",23],["eng12-u14-vocab",24]],"n":"Item 10 is missing from the printed exercise.","s":["1. respond","2. strangely","3. exhortation","4. substantiated","5. satisfaction","6. necessity (need)","7. successful","8. memorised","9. permitted","11. discoveries"]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-vocab",25],["eng12-u14-vocab",26],["eng12-u14-vocab",27],["eng12-u14-vocab",28],["eng12-u14-vocab",29],["eng12-u14-vocab",30],["eng12-u14-vocab",31],["eng12-u14-vocab",32],["eng12-u14-vocab",33],["eng12-u14-vocab",34],["eng12-u14-vocab",35],["eng12-u14-vocab",36],["eng12-u14-vocab",37]],"n":"Item 6 prints \"hormonica\" for harmonica.","s":["1. had asked, would have told","2. would have got, hadn't had (would not have got lost if we had had a map)","3. had had, would you have called","4. had been, would have bought","5. had known, would have come","6. would have sung, had played","7. had lived, would have learned","8. had rained, would have stayed","9. would have gone, had invited","10. had studied, would have become","11. had come, would have seen","12. What would Mary have done if she had been late for school?"]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-vocab",38]],"d":"eng12-u14-sentence-tick","s":["Part 1: The children wore their dirty boots — simple; It was time for bed, yet it was still light outside — compound; The front door was locked, so she entered through the back — compound; They took a lot of pictures when they visited Swat — complex; After the show began, he sat very quietly — complex; The girl was wearing a red dress — simple; He was playing on his computer — simple; Yahya Khan would like to play, for he is the best player on the team — compound; Before the bell rings, the students lined up at the door — complex; We turned off the lights — simple.","Part 2: We turned off the lights, and we went to sleep — compound; We turned off the lights after our mom told us to go to sleep — complex; I enjoyed the play, but I didn't like the end — compound; Jean was excited because her poem was greatly appreciated by all — complex; How fabulous our house looks with all the decoration! — simple (exclamatory); Will you please drop me at the community center? — simple (interrogative); Even if our team wins this match, we may not make it to the final — complex; Take the first right after 500 meters — simple (imperative); Dr. Asif prescribed some pills, and he told the patient to have a week's rest — compound; Dad met a man with whom he used to work — complex."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",1]],"s":["The narrator is a reporter (correspondent) travelling to the front during the war to report on it. Exhausted by the long journey, the snowstorm and the bitter cold, and because his truck was held up by a landslide, he decides to rest for a few hours at a roadside hut."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",2]],"s":["Cheng-yu is about ten or eleven years old. When asked why nobody is home, he answers that his father and mother are at work (his mother is at the depot/his father on the road), so he is alone at home."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",3]],"s":["His father works on the road gang as a demolition man, blasting rocks with explosives to keep the road through Lingkuan Gorge open; his mother works at the depot/warehouse."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",4]],"s":["His 'job' is to keep the stove burning and to watch the telephone (and wake his parents/answer calls) — his mother gave him this duty, and he takes it seriously."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",5]],"s":["He says he cannot go to sleep because it is his job to keep the fire going and watch the phone; his mother told him not to sleep, and if he slept he would not be doing his work."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",6]],"s":["In the final paragraph the narrator wakes up, sees the boy still awake and on duty, and sets out again. He realises that the road ahead is open because of the work of people like Cheng-yu's parents, and that a long, hard but hopeful journey — and his own duty — is waiting for him."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",7]],"s":["Cheng-yu is a responsible, brave, serious and mature child. He respects and obeys his parents, is proud of their work, and takes his small responsibility as seriously as an adult's job."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",8]],"s":["At first the narrator is tired, cold and gloomy, impatient at the delay. Meeting Cheng-yu changes his mood: the child's sense of duty and the hard work of the road builders fill him with warmth, hope and admiration, and he goes on his way inspired."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",9]],"s":["The story conveys that every job, however small, is important and should be done with dedication; that collective hard work builds a nation. It also values family responsibility, discipline, courage and the contribution of ordinary people."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u14-comprehension",11]],"s":["Sample story — Lingkuan Gorge: Beginning — During the war a reporter travelling to the front is stopped by a snowstorm and a landslide in the Lingkuan Gorge. Tired and cold, he goes to a small roadside hut to rest. Middle — Inside he finds a little boy, Cheng-yu, sitting alone by the stove. The boy explains that his father works on the road gang blasting rocks and his mother at the depot, and that his job is to keep the fire burning and watch the telephone. When the reporter tells him to sleep, he refuses because it is his duty. End — The reporter sleeps for a while and wakes to find the boy still awake. As dawn breaks and the road is cleared, he continues his journey, moved and inspired by the child's sense of responsibility."]},{"q":[["eng12-u14-vocab",1]],"s":["Discussion guide: groups list daily responsibilities at home — helping parents, keeping the room clean, buying groceries, looking after younger siblings, studying regularly, saving electricity and water — and discuss why sharing responsibilities is important, linking it to Cheng-yu's example."]}]}},"15":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u15-vocab-a",1],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",2],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",3],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",4],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",5],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",6],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",7],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",8],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",9],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",10],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",11],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",12],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",13],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",14],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",15],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",16],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",17],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",18],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",19],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",20],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",21],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",22],["eng12-u15-vocab-a",23],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",9],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",10]],"s":["1. b. harmful","2. b. beginner","3. c. desirable","4. d. cruel","5. d. selfish","6. b. make unimportant","7. c. disappeared","8. a. stooped with bent knees"]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",23],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",24],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",25],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",26],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",27],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",28],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",29],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",30],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",31],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",32],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",33],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",34],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",35],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",36],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",37],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",38],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",39],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",40],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",41],["eng12-u15-vocab-b",42],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",0],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",1],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",2],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",3],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",4],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",5]],"s":["1. will be leaving","2. will be having","3. will be driving","4. won't be taking","5. Where will you be sleeping tonight?","6. will be snowing","7. will be taking","8. will be feeling","9. will be missing","10. will be staying","11. will be sitting","12. will be working","13. will be having","14. will be appearing","15. will be playing"]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-vocab-c",7],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",8]],"s":["Sample sentences: 1. By 2075 Pakistan will have become a developed country. 2. Scientists will have found a cure for cancer. 3. People will have started living in smart cities. 4. Most cars will have switched to electricity. 5. People will have got more artificial by then. (Any five correct future perfect sentences.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-vocab-c",10],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",11],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",12],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",13],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",14],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",15],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",16],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",17],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",18],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",19],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",20],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",21],["eng12-u15-vocab-c",22],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",0],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",1],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",2],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",3],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",4],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",5],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",6],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",7],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",8],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",9],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",10],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",11],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",12],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",13],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",14],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",15]],"n":"Item 8 is missing from the printed exercise; item 9 fits the future perfect continuous better.","s":["1. will have repaired","2. will have done","3. will have visited","4. will have finished","5. will have arrested","6. will have played","7. will have taken","9. will have been sitting (will have sat)","10. will have left","11. will have discussed"]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-vocab-d",16],["eng12-u15-vocab-d",17]],"s":["Sample sentences (2040): 1. I will have been working as a doctor for 10 years. 2. I will have been living in Islamabad for 15 years. 3. I will have been serving my parents all these years. 4. I will have been teaching my children the Qur'an for five years. 5. I will have been writing articles for newspapers for 12 years. (Any five correct sentences.)"]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-vocab-e",1],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",2],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",3],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",4],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",5],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",6],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",7],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",8],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",9],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",10],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",11],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",12],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",13],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",14],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",15],["eng12-u15-vocab-e",16]],"s":["1. will have been sleeping","2. will have been running","3. Will you have been working","4. will have been waiting","5. will have been protesting","6. will have been teaching","7. Will he have been driving","8. living (will have been living)","9. will have been attending","10. will have been watching","11. will have been playing","12. How long will you have been practising guitar","13. will have been cooking","14. will have been learning","15. will have been playing havoc"]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",1]],"s":["In the past people laughed with their hearts and eyes, shook hands with their hearts, and were sincere and warm-hearted; guests were truly welcome. Today people laugh only with their teeth, shake hands without warmth, search your pockets with cold eyes, and say 'come again' without meaning it — modern people are insincere, artificial and selfish."]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",2]],"s":["The poet wants to learn from his young son how to laugh and smile genuinely again — to unlearn the artificial behaviour and false faces he has adopted, and to regain the innocence and sincerity of childhood."]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",3]],"s":["'Laugh with their teeth' means a false, artificial smile that shows only teeth and no real feeling. 'Teeth like a snake's bare fangs' means that such a smile is dangerous and deceitful — it hides hostility and harm behind a pleasant look."]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",4]],"s":["He compares their eyes to 'ice-block-cold eyes' — cold, cruel and lifeless — because modern people look at others without warmth, calculating and searching for their own benefit."]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",5]],"s":["The tone is nostalgic, regretful and ironic, with a note of sadness about the loss of sincerity; it ends in a hopeful, humble tone as the poet asks his son to teach him to laugh again."]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",6]],"s":["Stanza 1: 'they used to laugh with their hearts / laugh with their eyes' (metaphor of sincere laughter); 'ice-block-cold eyes' (metaphor). Stanza 4: 'I have learned to wear many faces like dresses' (simile) — home face, office face, street face, host face, cocktail face; 'conforming smiles like a fixed portrait smile' (simile)."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",8]],"n":"The poet (Gabriel Okara) died in 2019, so the stanzas are paraphrased, not quoted.","s":["Paraphrase of stanza 3: The poet says he has learned to say things he does not mean — 'Feel at home!' and 'Come again!' — and to shake hands and laugh without any real feeling. When guests come to his house again, he says the same words, but he does not wish them to come; it is only formal politeness.","Paraphrase of stanza 4: He says he has learned many things from society: to wear different faces like dresses for different places — at home, at the office, in the street, as a host and at parties — each with a fixed, artificial smile like a picture. He has also learned to laugh only with his teeth and to say 'Goodbye' when he means 'Good riddance'."]},{"q":[["eng12-u15-comprehension",10]],"s":["Debate guide — For modern times: technology, medicine, education, communication, comfort and opportunities. For the past: sincerity, strong family ties, simplicity, peace of mind, healthy food and environment, respect for elders. Each group should support points with examples; the teacher can conclude that we should combine modern progress with the sincerity of the past."]}]}},"16":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u16-vocab-a",1],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",2],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",3],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",4],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",5],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",6],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",7],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",8],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",9],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",10],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",11]],"s":["1. solemnity","2. tolerate","3. sufficiently","4. Familiarity","5. rebellious","6. inaugural","7. patriot","8. patriotism","9. glorious","10. stimulants"]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-vocab-a",12],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",13],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",14],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",15],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",16],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",17],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",18],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",19],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",20],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",21],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",22],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",23],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",24],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",25],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",26],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",27],["eng12-u16-vocab-a",28],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",0],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",1],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",2],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",3],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",4],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",5],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",6],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",7],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",8],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",9],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",10],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",11],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",12],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",13]],"n":"Item 11 prints \"expending\" for expanding and item 12 \"contact\" for contract.","s":["1. therefore / accordingly","2. nevertheless","3. otherwise","4. however / nevertheless","5. Accordingly (In response)","6. however","7. Since","8. Until","9. As soon as","10. moreover (actually)","11. Actually / Meanwhile","12. In order to","13. Whenever","14. therefore / accordingly","15. Meanwhile"]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-vocab-c",22],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",23],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",24],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",25],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",26],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",27],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",28],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",29],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",30],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",31],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",32],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",33],["eng12-u16-vocab-c",34],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",0],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",1],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",2],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",3],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",4]],"n":"Items 13–17 do not contain clear anaphors or cataphors in the italicised words; the most reasonable reading is given.","s":["1. They — anaphor; antecedent: Hurain and her parents","2. He — anaphor; antecedent: Hamad","3. he — cataphor; postcedent: the old washerman","4. It, their — anaphors; antecedents: a terrorist group, the villagers","5. his, his — cataphors; postcedent: an actor","6. it — anaphor; antecedent: the Qatar team","7. his — anaphor; antecedent: a person","8. she — anaphor; antecedent: any one of the sisters","9. he — anaphor; antecedent: someone","10. their — anaphor; antecedent: the pilot nor the attendants","11. its — anaphor; antecedent: each of these companies","12. your — refers to the reader (you); him — anaphor; antecedent: Umair","13. who was lazy — anaphoric (relative clause referring back to 'the boy')","14. I — cataphor; postcedent: your King","15. The flowers — antecedent of 'which' (anaphoric reference by which)","16. who live in glass houses — anaphoric (refers back to 'They')","17. is worth reading — 'this' / 'the only one' cataphorically points to the following description; 'one' refers back to 'poems'"]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-vocab-d",5],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",6],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",7],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",8],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",9],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",10],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",11],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",12],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",13],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",14],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",15],["eng12-u16-vocab-d",16]],"s":["1. Should you see John, please give him my best regards.","2. Were you to earn a huge salary, what would you do with the money?","3. Had he not squandered all his savings, he wouldn't be poor now.","4. Were she to get wind of the situation, she would definitely be devastated.","5. Should you need assistance, please let us know.","6. Should he be accepted for the job, he will go out and celebrate.","7. Had I not chosen to learn English, I wouldn't be doing this exercise right now.","8. Had it not been so strange, I would have remembered it.","9. Were I in your shoes, I would tell her the truth.","10. Were he promoted, his salary would double."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",2]],"s":["The world sees Pakistan as a country of great tourism potential: international travel magazines and agencies have ranked it among the best adventure and holiday destinations because of its mountains, history, culture and hospitality, although security concerns and poor facilities have kept tourist numbers low."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",3]],"s":["Natural attractions include the world's highest mountains (K-2, Nanga Parbat) and the Karakoram, Himalaya and Hindu Kush ranges; glaciers (Baltoro, Siachen); valleys like Swat, Kaghan, Hunza, Skardu and Chitral; lakes (Saiful Muluk, Attabad); deserts (Thar, Cholistan); and beaches of Makran and Karachi."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",4]],"s":["The Karakoram Highway follows part of the ancient Silk Route that linked China with South Asia and the West. Built jointly by Pakistan and China, it is one of the highest paved roads in the world and is called the 'Eighth Wonder of the World'; it is historically and economically important as a link between the two countries (and CPEC)."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",5]],"s":["Religious sites: for Muslims — the Badshahi Mosque, Faisal Mosque, shrines of Data Ganj Bakhsh and other saints; for Sikhs — Gurdwara Nankana Sahib (birthplace of Guru Nanak), Kartarpur and Panja Sahib; for Buddhists — Taxila, Takht-i-Bahi and Swat's stupas; for Hindus — Katas Raj and Hinglaj. They are significant as centres of faith, history and interfaith harmony."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",6]],"s":["Balochistan has the beautiful Makran coast and beaches (Gwadar, Kund Malir, Ormara), the Hingol National Park and Princess of Hope rock, Ziarat's juniper forests and Quaid's Residency, the ancient site of Mehrgarh, unique deserts and mountains — making it rich in natural and historical tourism."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",7]],"s":["We can improve Pakistan's image by ensuring security for tourists, building roads, hotels and facilities, easy online visas, promoting Pakistan on international media and social media, hosting international events, training tour guides, keeping sites clean, and showing our hospitality and culture to the world."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",8]],"s":["For Buddhists Pakistan holds the heritage of the Gandhara civilisation — Taxila, Takht-i-Bahi, Swat valley and Peshawar Museum with famous Buddha statues and stupas. For Sikhs it has their holiest places — Nankana Sahib, Kartarpur (connected by the Kartarpur Corridor), Panja Sahib at Hasan Abdal and Gurdwara Dera Sahib in Lahore. Religious tourism can attract millions of pilgrims."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",10]],"s":["Sample description — A Visit to Taxila: (Introduction with central idea) My visit to Taxila showed me how rich Pakistan's ancient heritage is. (Body) Last spring our class visited the Taxila Museum and the ruins of Sirkap and Dharmarajika. In the museum we saw stone statues of the Buddha, coins and jewellery of the Gandhara period. At Sirkap we walked along the ancient streets and saw the remains of houses and temples. The guide explained that Taxila was a great centre of learning 2,500 years ago. (Conclusion) The trip made me proud of our history and convinced me that such sites must be preserved and shown to the world."]},{"q":[["eng12-u16-comprehension",12]],"s":["Speaking game guide: each student writes two true statements and one false statement about himself (e.g., \"I have climbed Mount Elum\", \"I can play the flute\", \"I have three sisters\"); classmates ask questions to find the false one. It practises asking questions, listening and speaking confidently."]}]}},"17":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u17-vocab-a",1],["eng12-u17-vocab-a",2],["eng12-u17-vocab-a",3],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",8],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",9]],"s":["1. b) spacious","2. a) sociable","3. d) cowardly","4. a) urban","5. c) orally","6. a) withstand"]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-vocab-b",10],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",11],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",12],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",13],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",14],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",15],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",16],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",17],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",18],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",19],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",20],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",21],["eng12-u17-vocab-b",22],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",0],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",1],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",2],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",3],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",4],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",5],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",6],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",7],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",8],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",9],["eng12-u17-vocab-c",10],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",0],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",1],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",2],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",3],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",4],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",5],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",6],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",7],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",8],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",9],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",10],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",11],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",12],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",13],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",14],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",15],["eng12-u17-vocab-d",16],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",0],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",1],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",2],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",3],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",4],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",5],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",6],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",7],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",8],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",9],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",10],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",11],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",12],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",13],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",14],["eng12-u17-vocab-e",15]],"s":["Passage: observed; decided; caught; been; penalise; cancellation — \"It has been observed that drivers have become more careless … The traffic police committee has decided … the traffic offenders will be caught immediately … It has been decided to penalise the first instance … and the second with the cancellation of the driving licence.\"","Part B (Direct–Indirect speech) is explanatory: in indirect speech we remove the inverted commas, add 'that', change the person of pronouns, shift the tense back (am → was) and change adverbs of nearness (now → then, here → there, today → that day, tomorrow → the next day); questions use asked/inquired + if/whether with statement word order; commands use told/ordered/requested + to-infinitive."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-vocab-f",1],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",2],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",3],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",4],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",5],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",6],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",7],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",8],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",9],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",10],["eng12-u17-vocab-f",11]],"n":"Item 6 is missing from the printed exercise.","s":["1. Tahira said (that) she worked in an office.","2. Jamila told me (that) she was going to clean the room.","3. Jaffer said (that) they liked the song.","4. Iman and Marva remarked (that) they needed new shoes.","5. Kamran told us (that) he could speak Spanish.","7. The teacher said (that) Rauf had never been to Karachi.","8. Luqman said to Gul (that) the train hadn't arrived on time.","9. Eyma said (that) she was sitting on the chair.","10. Nauman told us (that) Waleed didn't eat meat.","11. Hamid remarked (that) Abrahim and Naummy would travel to Kaghan."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",1]],"s":["The central idea is the evil of racial prejudice and pride: Armand rejects his wife and child because he believes they are of mixed race, only to discover that the 'black blood' came from his own mother. The story shows how racism destroys love and humanity, and how pride blinds people."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",2]],"s":["Armand fell in love with Désirée at first sight, 'as if struck by a pistol shot'; his passion was so strong that he did not care about her unknown origin. He was proud of his own old and noble family name and felt he could give her his name, so her lack of family did not matter to him then."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",3]],"s":["Armand Aubigny is a rich, proud and passionate planter of an old Louisiana family. He is hot-tempered, harsh and cruel to his slaves; his love for Désirée is passionate but shallow. When he thinks his child is of mixed race, he becomes cold and cruel and drives Désirée away. He is a slave to racial prejudice and pride, and in the end he is shown to be the one with African ancestry."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",4]],"s":["His behaviour changed when the baby grew older and began to show features of African descent. Armand believed this came from Désirée, whose parentage was unknown; his racial pride was hurt, so he became cold, avoided her and the child and treated the slaves even more harshly."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",5]],"s":["Because Désirée was a foundling with no known family, while Armand was proud of his 'pure' white lineage. So the blame for the baby's dark features was put on her unknown origin — Armand never thought that the cause could be in his own family."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",6]],"s":["It means that Armand remembered, with sudden shame, that Désirée had no family name or known parentage; he felt she had brought disgrace to his proud and ancient name, and he blamed her nameless origin for the child's colour."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",7]],"s":["Alternate ending: Before Désirée leaves, Armand finds his mother's letter. Ashamed of his cruelty, he rushes to the bayou, brings Désirée and the baby back, asks her forgiveness and frees his slaves. He learns that love and humanity are more important than colour and race. (Any logical ending is accepted.)"]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",9]],"s":["Summary — Désirée's Baby: Beginning — Désirée, a foundling, is adopted by Monsieur and Madame Valmondé. When she grows up, the proud and wealthy Armand Aubigny falls in love with her and marries her, not caring about her unknown origin. Middle — They have a son and Armand is happy, but after some months the baby begins to show signs of African ancestry. Armand turns cold and cruel, and tells Désirée that the child is not white, which means she is not white. Heartbroken, Désirée takes the baby and disappears into the bayou. End — Later Armand burns all her belongings, and in a drawer he finds an old letter from his mother to his father, thanking God that Armand would never know that his mother belonged to the race 'cursed with the brand of slavery'. The truth is that Armand himself is of mixed race."]},{"q":[["eng12-u17-comprehension",11]],"s":["Discussion guide — forms of 'racism' in our society: discrimination on the basis of caste and biradari, tribe or ethnicity, language and province, rich and poor (class), skin colour in marriage proposals, urban and rural, sectarian differences. Each group gives examples and suggests remedies — the Islamic teaching of equality (Al-Hujurat 13), education, awareness and laws."]}]}},"18":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u18-vocab-a",1],["eng12-u18-vocab-a",2],["eng12-u18-vocab-a",3],["eng12-u18-vocab-a",4],["eng12-u18-vocab-a",5],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",0],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",1],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",2],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",3],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",4],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",5],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",6],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",7],["eng12-u18-vocab-b",8]],"s":["1. present","2. plump","3. glad","4. bright","5. below","6. hilarious","7. thief","8. ill","9. blend","10. price","11. aqua","12. error"]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-vocab-b",9]],"d":["eng12-u18-reported-1","eng12-u18-reported-2","eng12-u18-reported-3"],"n":"Item 3 prints \"It have worked\" (for I have worked) and item 29 \"You will have wait\" (will have to wait).","s":["1. He said that he worked in a small company.","2. She said that she was waiting for her salary.","3. Alex told me that he had worked very hard for that project.","4. I told him that I did not care about small issues.","5. She told me that she was making good progress on that project.","6. We said that they had a better future than us.","7. They said that it was getting very boring there.","8. She said that I had to decide by the next day.","9. Alex said that he had been writing that paragraph.","10. They said that they did not listen to their advice.","11. He said that he had worked very hard but had failed.","12. She told me that it had not worked for her.","13. Alex said that he had been listening to Indian music.","14. They said that they had been going to a new mission.","15. He said that he had collected ancient books.","16. They said that they had had a better alternative than that.","17. He told me that they had watched a wonderful movie.","18. Alex said that he had been writing codes for a new program.","19. She told him that she had had no intention to marry him.","20. He said that he had wasted his time after her.","21. She said that she would perform better in the next competition.","22. He told his teacher that he would bring good results in the exams.","23. She told her mother that she would bring a good name to her.","24. Alex told Mark that he would be waiting for him at the main gate.","25. I told him that I would have to convince my parents for that.","26. They said that they would have to stay more active.","27. She said that she would be looking for my response.","28. He told me that he would have to think twice before taking such action.","29. I told him that he would have to wait for me for two hours.","30. She said that they would be signing an agreement the next Sunday."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",1]],"s":["The title shows that the poem is an extract (lines) from Goldsmith's longer poem 'The Deserted Village'. It describes how a once happy and lively village, Auburn, has been deserted because the rich landlords seized the land and the peasants were forced to leave — the title reflects the poet's sorrow at this loss."]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",2]],"s":["The poet pictures 'sweet Auburn', the loveliest village of the plain, where health and plenty cheered the hard-working peasants; spring came early and summer stayed late; there were humble cottages, cultivated farms, a brook, a busy mill, a church on the hill and a hawthorn bush with seats beneath its shade for lovers and the old."]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",3]],"s":["(a) 'Loitered over thy green' — wandered leisurely over the village common/green fields. (b) 'Talking age' — the old people who sat together chatting. (c) 'Seats of my youth' — the places where the poet spent his happy childhood. (d) 'The village train' — the group/crowd of villagers who gathered for sports and games."]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",4]],"s":["The poem is written in heroic couplets — iambic pentameter lines rhyming in pairs; the rhyme scheme is AABBCC…"]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",5]],"s":["The land suffers greatly and falls a prey to quickly spreading evils where wealth gathers in the hands of a few rich people while the ordinary men (peasants) decline and suffer. Goldsmith means that a country is truly ruined when riches increase but the people who are its real strength are reduced to poverty and forced away."]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",6]],"s":["Examples: 'Sweet smiling village, loveliest of the lawn'; 'the busy mill'; 'decent church that topped the neighbouring hill'; 'Ill fares the land, to hastening ills a prey' (repetition of the l / h sounds)."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",8]],"s":["The poet calls the bold peasantry 'their country's pride' because they are the real backbone of a nation: they work hard, produce food, are honest, simple and healthy, and keep the country strong. Princes and lords can be made again by a king's word (\"a breath can make them, as a breath has made\"), but once a strong peasantry is destroyed, it can never be replaced."]},{"q":[["eng12-u18-comprehension",10]],"s":["Debate points — For modernisation of villages: electricity, roads, schools, hospitals, mobile networks, better farming methods, more income. Against: loss of simplicity, joint family system and community spirit, migration to cities, pollution, imitation of urban fashions. Speakers get 1–2 minutes each; the conclusion can stress progress while preserving good village values."]}]}},"19":{"lang":"en","items":{"objective":[{"q":[["eng12-u19-vocab",3],["eng12-u19-vocab",4],["eng12-u19-vocab",5],["eng12-u19-vocab",6],["eng12-u19-vocab",7],["eng12-u19-vocab",8],["eng12-u19-vocab",9],["eng12-u19-vocab",10],["eng12-u19-vocab",11],["eng12-u19-vocab",12],["eng12-u19-vocab",13],["eng12-u19-vocab",14],["eng12-u19-vocab",15]],"s":["1. antonyms","2. synonyms","3. antonyms","4. synonyms","5. synonyms","6. antonyms","7. antonyms","8. synonyms","9. antonyms","10. antonyms","11. antonyms","12. synonyms"]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-vocab",16],["eng12-u19-vocab",17],["eng12-u19-vocab",18],["eng12-u19-vocab",19],["eng12-u19-vocab",20],["eng12-u19-vocab",21],["eng12-u19-vocab",22],["eng12-u19-vocab",23],["eng12-u19-vocab",24],["eng12-u19-vocab",25],["eng12-u19-vocab",26]],"s":["1. complex","2. simple (compound predicate)","3. complex","4. simple","5. simple","6. simple","7. simple","8. compound","9. simple","10. complex"]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-vocab",27],["eng12-u19-vocab",28],["eng12-u19-vocab",29],["eng12-u19-vocab",30],["eng12-u19-vocab",31],["eng12-u19-vocab",32],["eng12-u19-vocab",33],["eng12-u19-vocab",34],["eng12-u19-vocab",35]],"n":"In item 4 neither word is clearly stronger; whispered is the more specific (and in item 3 \"glass plated\" is printed for glass plate).","s":["1. beautiful","2. delicious","3. crashed","4. whispered (as the 'stronger' choice is unclear, 'said' is neutral; 'whispered' is more specific)","5. starving","6. hard","7. dash","8. furious"]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-vocab",36]],"d":["eng12-u19-reported-1","eng12-u19-reported-2","eng12-u19-reported-3"],"n":"The book's back matter (authors' profile, feedback form) follows this activity and is not part of the exercise.","s":["1. He said that he was working hard for the upcoming tests.","2. She said that her only wish was to become a good person.","3. He said that he was learning the rules of a new language.","4. I told her that she seemed to be a nice person.","5. He told me that I should be responsible in my duty.","6. Asad said that it was a rule to obey the master.","7. They said that they were waiting for their brothers.","8. I said that I was the winner in that game.","9. They said that they were learning English by themselves.","10. He ordered his servant to clean the tables again.","11. Mother angrily ordered her daughter to be quiet and go into her room.","12. He asked the waiter to bring him the detailed bill.","13. He told Raja to close the door.","14. Father told his son to get ready as they would leave quickly.","15. I requested the policemen to help the poor lady.","16. The old man requested them to release his son that time.","17. She told him never to try to talk to her.","18. The teacher ordered all the students to stand up.","19. He advised his son to avoid sitting with bad company.","20. The doctor advised him to stay away from junk food.","21. I suggested that we should let him sleep before we left.","22. She suggested to him that they should meet her parents somewhere.","23. I asked her to let me think before I gave her any proposal.","24. She asked her father to let her buy some books for Alex.","25. I suggested that we should start reading that novel together.","26. The teacher suggested that they should start over a new chapter.","27. She asked to be allowed to be more prepared for the task.","28. I said that I wanted to find the best solution to solve that issue permanently.","29. They suggested playing a game where the winner would win a gold medal.","30. She requested me to let her stay there until her brother came."]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",2]],"s":["During a war the boys were being evacuated from Britain by plane; the plane was shot down/crashed on an uninhabited tropical island, and the boys survived with no adults."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",3]],"s":["Ralph found a large conch shell in the lagoon and, with Piggy's help, blew it like a trumpet; the loud sound called all the scattered boys together to one place for a meeting."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",4]],"s":["Jack and his choir boys came marching along the beach in two lines, dressed in black cloaks and caps with silver badges, looking like a strange dark creature in the heat; Jack, tall and red-haired, led them with authority and they halted in order before the assembly."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",5]],"s":["The first issue was choosing a leader. They held a vote; Ralph, who had the conch, was elected chief, while Jack was given charge of his choir as hunters to satisfy him."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",6]],"s":["Ralph believed they would be rescued if they kept a signal fire burning on the mountain so that a passing ship would see the smoke; he also hoped his father, a naval officer, would come for them."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",7]],"s":["The rule was that whoever held the conch had the right to speak in the meeting and others must listen; it was made to keep order, give everyone a fair chance and make the assemblies democratic."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",8]],"s":["In the hallucination scene Simon, alone in the forest, imagines the pig's head on the stick (the Lord of the Flies) speaking to him. It tells him that the 'beast' is not outside but inside the boys themselves. The scene reveals the novel's main idea about the evil within human nature."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",9]],"s":["'Lord of the Flies' is a translation of 'Beelzebub', a name of the devil. It refers to the pig's head on a stick swarming with flies, which the boys offer to the beast. The title symbolises the evil and savagery that exist within human beings."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",10]],"s":["Roger rolls a huge rock that kills Piggy and shatters the conch into pieces. It suggests the complete end of order, reason and civilisation on the island and the victory of savagery."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",11]],"s":["Jack's tribe sets the island on fire while hunting Ralph. The smoke attracts a passing naval ship; an officer lands on the beach just as Ralph falls at his feet, and the boys are rescued."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",12]],"s":["Ralph's priorities were order, building shelters, keeping the signal fire and being rescued. Jack cared only about hunting, meat, power and fun; he ignored the fire and rules, which led to the split between them."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",13]],"s":["Simon represents natural goodness and spiritual insight — he is kind, helpful and thoughtful, and he understands that the beast is within the boys; some critics see him as a Christ-like figure."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",14]],"s":["Both Ralph and Piggy stand for order and civilisation. Ralph is athletic, attractive and a natural leader, but less intelligent; Piggy is fat, asthmatic and short-sighted, mocked by others, but he is the most intelligent and logical. Ralph provides leadership, Piggy provides ideas."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",15]],"s":["The mock-hunts show how the boys' games gradually become real violence; chanting \"Kill the pig!\" they lose control, attack Robert and later kill Simon — showing the growth of savagery in human nature."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",16]],"s":["These are Jack's words. On the occasion of his feast after leaving Ralph's group, he invites the boys to join his tribe, promising them meat, hunting and protection from the beast — he is trying to win followers away from Ralph."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",17]],"s":["At the feast Jack sits like an idol, painted and garlanded, with power resting on his shoulders. Golding shows that Jack has now become a tyrant ruler; his authority comes from fear and the boys' desire for meat and excitement, not from law or reason."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",18]],"s":["These are Ralph's words. The statement is true in the story: the fire is the boys' only link with civilisation and hope of rescue; when they neglect it, they lose that hope, and ironically it is a fire (the burning island) that finally brings rescue."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",19]],"s":["The dominant theme is the conflict between civilisation and savagery — the instinct to live by rules and morality versus the instinct to seek power and violence. Without the restraints of society, the evil within human nature comes out."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",20]],"s":["The novel suggests that children are not naturally innocent; they have the same capacity for cruelty, fear and lust for power as adults, and without guidance and rules they can easily turn savage."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",21]],"s":["Symbols: the conch shell — order, democracy and authority; Piggy's glasses — intelligence, science and reason (also the means of making fire); the signal fire — hope of rescue and link with civilisation; the beast — the evil and fear inside human beings."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",22]],"s":["The conch shell symbolises law and democratic order; Piggy's glasses stand for reason and the power of science; the signal fire stands for hope and the connection with civilisation (when it goes out, hope fades); the beast represents the inner evil and savagery of mankind, which the boys wrongly think is outside them."]}],"activities":[{"q":[["eng12-u19-comprehension",24],["eng12-u19-comprehension",25]],"s":["1. Character sketches — Ralph: elected leader, fair, sensible, stands for order and rescue; he weakens as savagery grows but keeps his humanity. Jack: proud, aggressive head of the choir who becomes a cruel dictator; he represents the lust for power and savagery. Piggy: intelligent, practical, short-sighted and weak; represents reason and science; mocked and finally killed. Simon: quiet, kind and spiritual; understands the true nature of the beast; killed by the boys in a frenzy.","2. Ralph and Jack compared: both are natural leaders, but Ralph leads by consent and rules while Jack rules by fear and force; Ralph wants shelter, fire and rescue, Jack wants hunting, meat and power; Ralph represents civilisation, Jack represents savagery."]},{"q":[["eng12-u19-vocab",1]],"s":["Role-play guide: the 'Ralph group' proposes order-based solutions (rules, sharing work, keeping the fire, holding meetings with the conch); the 'Jack group' proposes force-based solutions (hunting, a strong leader, punishment). After presenting proposals, the class discusses which approach better solves problems and why democratic order is essential."]}]}}});
